\section{Introduction}
\label{sect:1}

The automatic Report has the goal to provide to ESO the performances assessment of the FATE project i.e. a picture of the characteristics of the forecasts delivered to ESO in operative configuration in the context of the FATE project. The report is supposed to be delivered to ESO with a monthly frequency. In agreement with ESO it has been decided to include in the report model performances following specific figures of merit as well as the failures experienced by FATE, ESO or ECMWF during the process of forecast production and delivery. The three institutes represent the nodes of the automatic system. The figures of merit used to quantify the forecasts performances have been identified in agreement with ESO. Forecasts products delivered to ESO follow contractual specifications (SoW) and contract documentation. \\ \\
\noindent
For the night time FATE is supposed to provide forecasts related to 8 parameters: wind speed (WS) and direction (WD), relative humidity (RH), precipitable water vapour (PWV), seeing ($\varepsilon$), wavefront coherence time ($\tau_{0}$), ground layer fraction (GLF), sky transparency. \\ \\
For the day time, FATE is supposed to provide forecasts related to 5 parameters: wind speed (WS) and direction (WD), relative humidity (RH), precipitable water vapour (PWV), sky transparency.\\ \\
\noindent
The sky transparency is provided with the operative General Circulation Model of ECMWF at high resolution (HRES). For all the other parameters FATE is supposed to provide forecasts at long time scale and at short time scale obtained with the Meso-Nh model and Astro-Meso-Nh model. The latter is the package for the optical turbulence and all integrated parameters depending on the $C_{N}^{2}$. FATE allows different forecast time scales (FTS). All forecasts at long time scales are delivered within two hours before the sunset (night) and before the sunrise (day) as requested by technical specifications.  Forecasts at short time scale are calculated using an autoregression approach (called AR hereafter) proposed in \cite{masciadri2020} and applied to the case of VLT \cite{masciadri2023}. The AR implies that, at each full time hour, FATE provides the forecasts for the coming 4h of all the three astroclimatic parameters ($\varepsilon$, $\tau_{0}$ and GLF) plus the forecasts of four atmospheric parameters (WS, WD, RH and PWV). In other words, the forecasts are updated at each full time hour related in the night and day time provided the conditions for the calculation are respected\footnote{We refer the reader to \cite{tech_note} for the these technicalities}. We refer the reader to the paper \cite{masciadri2023} for the model configuration, the definition of parameters and the explanation of the AR method. Forecasts at long time scales are calculated with a time scale of 1 night, 2 nights, 3 nights as well as for 1 day, 2 days, 3 days and results are delivered 2 hours before sunset (for night time) and 2 hours before sunrise (for day time) following the configuration described in the document \cite{for_scheme} located in the dedicated ESO repository\footnote{\href{https://drive.google.com/drive/u/0/folders/1K1nq32EgTrXc5MTKEELHhY1EhGY9QxqE}{https://drive.google.com/drive/u/0/folders/1K1nq32EgTrXc5MTKEELHhY1EhGY9QxqE}. Angel Otarola and Steffen Mieske are the ESO contact point for the FATE project. The repository is not public. }.  For the short time scales, in agreement with ESO, the report shows results related to the FTS at 1h that is the time scale more relevant for ESO for the VLT science operation. \\ \\ 
\noindent
Analysis of forecast performances is done using two different approaches: 
\begin{itemize}

\item calculation of bias, RMSE and $\sigma$ applied to the scatter plots between observations and forecasts. In \cite{masciadri2023} are reported  definitions as well as the description of how data are treated. 

\item calculation of contingency tables (3$\times$3 for all the parameters with exception of WD treated with a 4$\times$4 table) and derived parameters: percentage of corrected forecasts (PC), extremely bad forecasts (EBD), probability of detection (POD$_{1,2,3}$). See \cite{masciadri2023}-Appendix and \cite{lascaux2015} for the definition and for an extended digression on these parameters.

\end{itemize}
\noindent
Temporal frequency of sampling for observations and forecasts, in the standard configuration as well as in the AR case, is 10 minutes as requested by the ESO technical specifications. \\ \\
{\vskip 1cm}
\noindent
{\bf LEGENDA}\\ \\
\noindent
- {\bf Standard configuration}:  we mean with that those forecasts at long time scales (1 night/day, 2 nights/days, 3 nights/days) performed with an atmospherical model only having a forecast time scale (FTS) of [22h-33h], [47h-57h] and [71h-81h] respectively.  Model configuration for the case 1 night is described in detail in \cite{masciadri2023} and \cite{for_scheme}. Extrapolation to the other cases (2 nights/days and 3 nights/days) is reported in \cite{for_scheme}.\\ \\
- {\bf AR}:  we mean with that the auto-regression method (\cite{masciadri2020,masciadri2023}). Such a method is a hybrid method taking into account the forecasts performed with the atmospherical model plus the real-time observations related to a finite number of nights in the close past. In \cite{masciadri2020} it has been proven that the optimal number of nights in the past is N+5. Both terms (observations and forecasts from the atmospherical model) are treated with a statistical algorithm, more precisely an auto-regression algorithm \cite{masciadri2020}. The method has been already applied in the forecast of the PWV at short time scale at VLT \cite{turchi2020}. \\ \\
\noindent
In the report data analysis is reported using the following criterium.  Each chapter of the report treats the cases: 1 night/day, 2 nights/days, 3 nights/days. In the case of 1 night/day the long FTS (standard configuration) as well as the short FTS (AR) are treated while, in the cases 2 nights/days and 3 nights/days, only the long FTS (standard configuration) is treated. Each main chapter has three sub-sections:\\ 
\begin{itemize}

\item The first sub-section (ex: 2.1) reports, for all the parameters, the scatter plots of the standard configuration, the AR at a time scale of 1h and the prediction by persistence a time scale of 1h. For the seeing, we considered observations only $<$ 1.5''. This is because it is known that performances with the standard configuration decrease as soon as the value of the parameters increases. Due to the fact that AO can hardly be used for $\varepsilon$ $>$ 1.5" it is considered useless (or weakly interesting) to analyse performances for seeing $>$ 1.5" .  Definition of the prediction by persistence is given in \cite{commis_prob}. It synthesis, it assumes that the value of the parameter P at a time t (i.e. P(t)) for whatever t $>$ t$_{0}$ is equal to the mean of P calculated in the 10 minutes before t$_{0}$.   \\

\item In the second sub-section (ex: 2.2), values of bias, RMSE and $\sigma$ are reported in tables of synthesis. To facilitate the analysis, tables show the statistics obtained in the last month as well as the statistics obtained since the beginning of the operational phase (called 'incremental month'). The latter is obviously more representative from a statistical point of view and we expect that the latter case should approach the statistics obtained on a long time scale (12 months i.e. a whole solar year) in the context of a research approach \cite{masciadri2023}. Tests The statistics performed on a time scale of the current month can present some fluctuations and it is less representative in statistical terms. This has been verified in the commissioning. \\

\item The third sub-section (ex: 2.3) reports the following statistical operators values (PC, EBD, POD$_{1,2,3}$) retrieved from the contingency tables calculated for each atmospheric and astroclimatic parameter. For each parameters we used, as thresholds of the contingency tables, the first and second tertiles calculated off-line on a climatological time scale, more precisely on a time scale of 1 solar year [1/8/2018-31/7/2019] (the period used in the paper \cite{masciadri2023}). Values of first and second tertiles for all the paramaters are reported in the documented \cite{tech_specif}. The WD is the unique parameter that requires a 4$\times$4 table. In that case the thresholds selected have been the directions: [45$^{circle}$-225$^{circle}$] and [135$^{circle}$-315$^{circle}$] identifying the sectors: North (N), East (E), South (S) and West (W) (see \cite{lascaux2015} Fig.3). In the calculation of the contingency tables, for the astroclimatic parameters, (i.e. those parameters depending on the $C_{N}^{2}$), two different options are taken into account: \\  \\
\noindent
{\bf (A)} the simple and classical contingency table in which are reported the number of couples (observation, forecast) falling in the each box. In that case the reference is, depending on the parameter, measurement provided by different instrument. See \cite{masciadri2023} for details. In syntesis: DIMM for the seeing, MASS-DIMM for $\tau_{0}$ and GLF. Automatic weather station for all the atmospheric paramaters.\\ \\
{\bf (B)} a modified contingency tables in which the coincidence in the main diagonal is done taking into account a realistic accuracy for the estimate of the individual parameter. The accuracy is estimated with the $\sigma$ calculated between instruments measuring the same parameter at the same time. We refer the read to \cite{masciadri2023} for an extended discussion on that topic. The uncertainties (or accuracies) have been calculated on a very rich statistical samples. The FATE forecasts of astroclimatic parameters are supposed to be used to manage science operations of the whole VLT i.e. the four UT as well as the interferometry obtained with UT and AT. That means that is indeed poorly representative an estimate that does not take into account an accuracy/uncertainty. \\ 
\end{itemize}
\noindent
Contingency tables are reported in an Annex that is delivered, jointly with the Report, on a monthly time scale.\\ \\
\noindent
{\it At the end of the commissioning [2023/09/01 - 2023/12/31] we discovered an effect (that we call hereafter "border effect") that degrades performances of forecasts in AR configuration and they are worse than what we expected. The bad news is that performances of the AR mode appear therefore less good that what it has been estimated in \cite{masciadri2023} even if the substantial improvement with respect to the standard configuration is evident. The good news is that we identified during the commissioning the cause of this effect. The main priority of the R\&D activity of the FATE project will be therefore focused on looking for a solution to this problem in order to improve these results. An explanation of this effect and an explanation of the causes of this effect is reported in \cite{tech_note}.}


 

